Use `optimize()` with GEPA and a multi-objective metric that returns `{ accuracy, brevity }` — GEPA builds a Pareto front so you can pick the best accuracy/length trade-off, rather than collapsing both into one number.

```typescript
import { ai, ax, optimize, AxAIOpenAIModel } from '@ax-llm/ax';

const student = ai({
  name: 'openai',
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_APIKEY!,
  config: { model: AxAIOpenAIModel.GPT54Mini }, // cheap, runs the actual classifier
});

const teacher = ai({
  name: 'openai',
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_APIKEY!,
  config: { model: AxAIOpenAIModel.GPT54 }, // stronger, proposes prompt edits
});

const classifier = ax(
  'ticketText:string -> category:class "billing, bug, feature, account, other", rationale:string'
);

// split your ~200 examples: train for optimization, validation as holdout
const train = labeledExamples.slice(0, 150);
const validation = labeledExamples.slice(150);

const metric = ({ prediction, example }: { prediction: any; example: any }) => {
  const accuracy = prediction?.category === example?.category ? 1 : 0;
  const rationale = typeof prediction?.rationale === 'string' ? prediction.rationale : '';
  const brevity =
    rationale.length <= 40 ? 1 : rationale.length <= 80 ? 0.5 : 0.1;
  return { accuracy, brevity };
};

const result = await optimize(classifier, train, metric, {
  studentAI: student,
  teacherAI: teacher,
  numTrials: 16,
  minibatch: true,
  minibatchSize: 6,
  earlyStoppingTrials: 5,
  validationExamples: validation,
  maxMetricCalls: 240,
});

// paretoFront holds every accuracy/brevity trade-off found
for (const point of result.paretoFront) {
  console.log(point.scores, point.configuration);
}

classifier.applyOptimization(result.optimizedProgram!); // picks the best overall candidate
console.log(result.bestScore);
```

Notes:
- `metric` returning an object (not a number) is what triggers Pareto/multi-objective scoring in GEPA.
- Keep `train`/`validation` disjoint — don't reuse training rows for validation.
- `maxMetricCalls` must cover at least one full validation pass plus all trial rounds; 240 is generous for 200 examples at 16 trials.
- If you want a single best-of, `result.optimizedProgram` already balances both objectives; inspect `paretoFront` if you'd rather choose the trade-off manually (e.g. favor accuracy over brevity).

Use `optimize()` with a **multi-objective metric** (accuracy + length penalty) and let GEPA's Pareto search trade them off — no separate length-tuning pass needed.

Split your 200 examples ~140 train / 60 validation, then:

```typescript
import { ai, ax, optimize, AxAIOpenAIModel } from '@ax-llm/ax';

const student = ai({
  name: 'openai',
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_APIKEY!,
  config: { model: AxAIOpenAIModel.GPT54Mini },
});

const teacher = ai({
  name: 'openai',
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_APIKEY!,
  config: { model: AxAIOpenAIModel.GPT54 },
});

const classifier = ax(
  'ticketText:string -> category:class "billing, bug, account, feature_request, other", reason:string'
);

// train: ~140 examples, validation: ~60 held-out examples
// each: { ticketText: string, category: string }

const metric = ({ prediction, example }: { prediction: any; example: any }) => {
  const accuracy = prediction?.category === example?.category ? 1 : 0;
  const reason = typeof prediction?.reason === 'string' ? prediction.reason : '';
  const brevity =
    reason.length <= 60 ? 1 : reason.length <= 120 ? 0.5 : 0.1;
  return { accuracy, brevity };
};

const result = await optimize(classifier, train, metric, {
  studentAI: student,
  teacherAI: teacher,
  numTrials: 16,
  minibatch: true,
  minibatchSize: 6,
  earlyStoppingTrials: 5,
  sampleCount: 1,
  validationExamples: validation,
  maxMetricCalls: 240,
});

// Inspect trade-offs across the Pareto front, then pick a point
for (const point of result.paretoFront) {
  console.log(point.scores, point.configuration);
}

classifier.applyOptimization(result.optimizedProgram!);
console.log(result.bestScore);
```

Key points:
- The metric returns an **object** (`{ accuracy, brevity }`), which signals GEPA to run true multi-objective (Pareto) optimization instead of collapsing to one score.
- `optimize()` first runs `AxBootstrapFewShot` (picks good few-shot demos from `train`) then `AxGEPA` (evolves the instruction text), so both prompt wording and demos get tuned.
- Use `result.paretoFront` to see candidates ranked by both objectives and manually pick a preferred trade-off if the auto-selected `optimizedProgram` leans too far one way.
- Persist the winner with `axSerializeOptimizedProgram(result.optimizedProgram)` so you don't have to re-run optimization on every deploy.

Use `optimize()` with `AxGEPA` and a multi-objective metric that returns `{ accuracy, brevity }`. GEPA will search the Pareto front of prompts that balance both, and you apply the best trade-off candidate to your generator.

```typescript
import { ai, ax, optimize, AxAIOpenAIModel } from '@ax-llm/ax';

// Cheaper model actually running the classifier
const student = ai({
  name: 'openai',
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_APIKEY!,
  config: { model: AxAIOpenAIModel.GPT54Mini },
});

// Stronger model proposing prompt improvements
const teacher = ai({
  name: 'openai',
  apiKey: process.env.OPENAI_APIKEY!,
  config: { model: AxAIOpenAIModel.GPT54 },
});

const classifier = ax(
  'ticketText:string -> category:class "billing, bug, account, feature_request, other", reasoning:string'
);

// Split your ~200 labeled examples: train for optimization, held-out for validation
const allExamples = [
  { ticketText: 'Card was charged twice this month', category: 'billing' },
  { ticketText: 'App crashes when I upload a photo', category: 'bug' },
  // ... ~200 total
];
const train = allExamples.slice(0, 160);
const validation = allExamples.slice(160);

// Multi-objective metric: correctness + short reasoning
const metric = ({ prediction, example }: { prediction: any; example: any }) => {
  const accuracy = prediction?.category === example?.category ? 1 : 0;
  const reasoning = typeof prediction?.reasoning === 'string' ? prediction.reasoning : '';
  const brevity =
    reasoning.length <= 60 ? 1 : reasoning.length <= 120 ? 0.5 : 0.1;
  return { accuracy, brevity };
};

const result = await optimize(classifier, train, metric, {
  studentAI: student,
  teacherAI: teacher,
  numTrials: 16,
  minibatch: true,
  minibatchSize: 8,
  earlyStoppingTrials: 5,
  sampleCount: 1,
  validationExamples: validation,
  maxMetricCalls: 250,
});

// Inspect trade-offs across the Pareto front
for (const point of result.paretoFront) {
  console.log(point.scores, point.configuration);
}

// Apply the best overall candidate
classifier.applyOptimization(result.optimizedProgram!);
console.log(result.bestScore);

// Persist for reuse
const saved = JSON.stringify(result.optimizedProgram);
```

Key points:
- Return an object (`{ accuracy, brevity }`) from the metric, not a scalar — that's what triggers Pareto/multi-objective optimization in GEPA instead of single-score hill-climbing.
- Keep `train`/`validationExamples` disjoint; use ~80/20 of your 200 examples.
- `paretoFront` gives you multiple prompts at different accuracy/brevity trade-offs if you want to pick manually instead of taking `optimizedProgram` (GEPA's default best pick).
- `maxMetricCalls` must cover the validation pass plus trial rounds — bump it up if you see a sizing error.

