**Durable Object — accept + hibernate:**
```ts
export class Board extends DurableObject<Env> {
  async fetch(req: Request) {
    const { 0: client, 1: server } = new WebSocketPair();
    // hibernatable accept — DO can evict while socket stays open
    this.ctx.acceptWebSocket(server, [/* tags, e.g. userId */]);
    return new Response(null, { status: 101, webSocket: client });
  }

  // Called on wake (fresh instance OR still-warm), no persistent
  // in-memory listener needed
  async webSocketMessage(ws: WebSocket, message: string) {
    const op = JSON.parse(message); // e.g. a CRDT/diff op
    this.ctx.storage.sql.exec(
      "INSERT INTO ops (data) VALUES (?)", message
    );
    // fan out to every other connected client
    for (const peer of this.ctx.getWebSockets()) {
      if (peer !== ws) peer.send(message);
    }
  }

  async webSocketClose(ws: WebSocket, code: number) {
    ws.close(code, "closing");
  }
}
```

Key points:
- `acceptWebSocket` (not `server.accept()`) is what enables hibernation — the runtime detaches JS state and only wakes the DO on an actual message/close/error.
- `ctx.getWebSockets()` recovers all currently-attached sockets after a wake, so you don't need to persist a connection list yourself.
- Persist ops/state via `ctx.storage.sql` (SQLite-backed DO) before or as you broadcast, so a newly-hibernated-then-woken instance (or a fresh cold instance if truly idle long enough) can replay board state to a late joiner.
- No alarms/timers needed just to "keep it alive" — hibernation means an idle board with open tabs costs ~nothing until someone draws.

For late joiners, send a snapshot (from SQLite) right after `acceptWebSocket` rather than replaying the whole op log, and keep `webSocketMessage` handlers synchronous where possible — avoid `blockConcurrencyWhile` on the hot path.

